Here's how NexiFuse approaches this: To implement an LRU (Least Recently Used) cache in C#, you can use the `Dictionary` class to store key-value pairs, where each key is a unique identifier for the cached item and the value is the actual data being stored. You'll also need to keep track of the order in which items were accessed using a separate data structure.

Here's an example implementation:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class LRUCache<T>
{
    private Dictionary<string, T> _cache = new Dictionary<string, T>();
    private List<string> _accessOrder = new List<string>();

    public void Add(string key, T value)
    {
        if (_cache.ContainsKey(key))
            Remove(key);

        _cache.AddOrUpdate(key, value);
        _accessOrder.Insert(0, key); // Insert at the beginning to maintain order
    }

    public T Get(string key)
    {
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(key, out T value))
        {
            // Update access order when retrieving an item
            _accessOrder.Insert(1, key); // Move to second position for LRU behavior
            return value;
        }
        else
        {
            // Item not found in cache; you can choose to evict the least recently used or remove it entirely
            Remove(_accessOrder[0]);
            return default(T);
        }
    }

    public void Remove(string key)
    {
        _cache.Remove(key);
        _accessOrder.Remove(key); // Remove from access order as well
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a `Dictionary` to store the cache items and a separate `_accessOrder` list to keep track of the order in which items were accessed. The `Add()` method adds an item to the cache, updating its position at the beginning of the `_accessOrder` list. The `Get()` method retrieves an item from the cache, moving it to the second position (to maintain LRU behavior). If an item is not found in the cache, it's removed from the cache and access order.

Note that this implementation has a time complexity of O(n log n) due to the insertion at the beginning of the `_accessOrder` list using `Insert(0, key)` which takes O(log n) time.

