Here's an example of implementing Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithm in Java:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Node {
    int value;
    List<Node> neighbors = new ArrayList<>();

    public Node(int val) {
        this.value = val;
    }
}

public class BFS {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a graph (nodes and edges)
        Node node1 = new Node(1);
        Node node2 = new Node(2);
        Node node3 = new Node(3);
        Node node4 = new Node(4);

        node1.neighbors.add(node2);
        node1.neighbors.add(node3);
        node2.neighbors.add(node4);

        // Initialize the queue
        Queue<Node> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.add(node1);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            Node current = queue.poll();

            System.out.println("Visited: " + current.value);

            for (Node neighbor : current.neighbors) {
                if (!visited.contains(neighbor)) { // Mark as visited
                    visited.add(neighbor);
                    queue.add(neighbor);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private static Set<Node> visited = new HashSet<>();
}
```

This code creates a graph with four nodes and two edges. The BFS algorithm is implemented using a queue to keep track of the nodes to visit next.

The time complexity of this implementation is O(n log n), where n is the number of nodes in the graph, because we use an ArrayList (which has logarithmic time complexity for adding elements) to store the neighbors of each node.

