Here's an example of implementing Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithm in JavaScript:

```javascript
function bfs(graph, startNode) {
    let visited = new Set();
    let queue = [[startNode]];

    while (queue.length > 0) {
        let currentLevel = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
            let node = graph[queue[i]];
            if (!visited.has(node)) {
                visited.add(node);
                currentLevel.push(...node);
            }
        }
        queue = [currentLevel];
    }

    return visited;
}
```

In this implementation, we use a `Set` to keep track of visited nodes and an array (`queue`) to store the nodes at each level. We iterate through the queue until it's empty, processing each node by adding its neighbors to the next level in the queue. This ensures that we visit all nodes at each level before moving on to the next one.

The time complexity is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the graph, because we visit each node exactly once. The space complexity is also O(n) due to the storage requirements for the `visited` set and the queue array.

