Here's an example of how to implement binary search in C#:

**Binary Search Algorithm**

The binary search algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half and searching for the target value in one of the two halves. This process is repeated until the target value is found or it's determined that it's not present in the array.

Here's a simple implementation of binary search with O(n^2) complexity:
```csharp
public static int BinarySearch(int[] arr, int target)
{
    int low = 0;
    int high = arr.Length - 1;

    while (low <= high)
    {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2; // Calculate the middle index

        if (arr[mid] == target)
        {
            return mid; // Target found, return the index
        }
        else if (arr[mid] < target)
        {
            low = mid + 1; // Search in the upper half
        }
        else
        {
            high = mid - 1; // Search in the lower half
        }
    }

    return -1; // Target not found
}
```
**O(n^2) Complexity**

The binary search algorithm has a time complexity of O(log n), which is much better than the O(n^2) complexity mentioned earlier. However, if you want to implement an O(n^2) solution for some reason (e.g., educational purposes or testing), here's how:

```csharp
public static int LinearSearch(int[] arr, int target)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.Length; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] == target)
        {
            return i; // Target found, return the index
        }
    }

    return -1; // Target not found
}
```
In this implementation, we iterate through the array once and check each element to see if it matches the target value. This has a time complexity of O(n), which is actually better than binary search's O(log n) complexity.

Please note that in most cases, you should use the more efficient binary search algorithm with O(log n) complexity.

