Here's an example of how to implement Dijkstra's algorithm in TypeScript:

```typescript
interface Node {
  id: number;
  distance: number;
}

function dijkstra(graph: { [key: number]: { [key: number]: number } }, startNodeId: number) {
  const nodes = Object.keys(graph).map((nodeId, index) => ({
    id: parseInt(nodeId),
    distance: Infinity
  }));

  const distances = new Map();
  distances.set(startNodeId, 0);

  while (true) {
    let minDistance = Infinity;
    let currentNodeId = -1;

    for (const [nodeId, node] of nodes.entries()) {
      if (!distances.has(node.id)) continue;

      if (node.distance < minDistance) {
        minDistance = node.distance;
        currentNodeId = node.id;
      }
    }

    if (currentNodeId === -1) break;

    for (const [neighborNodeId, weight] of Object.entries(graph[currentNodeId])) {
      const newDistance = distances.get(currentNodeId) + weight;

      if (!distances.has(neighborNodeId)) {
        distances.set(neighborNodeId, newDistance);
      } else if (newDistance < distances.get(neighborNodeId)) {
        distances.set(neighborNodeId, newDistance);
      }
    }

    nodes[index].distance = minDistance;
  }

  return Array.from(nodes.entries()).map(([id, node]) => ({ id: id, distance: node.distance }));
}
```

This implementation uses a simple array to represent the graph and iterates over it using a while loop. The algorithm maintains two data structures:

1. `nodes`: An array of objects representing each node in the graph.
2. `distances`: A Map that keeps track of the shortest distance from the start node to each other node.

The algorithm initializes the distances for all nodes except the start node to infinity, and sets the distance for the start node to 0. Then it enters a loop where it finds the node with the smallest distance (i.e., the one closest to the start node), updates its neighbors' distances if necessary, and repeats this process until no more improvements can be made.

This implementation has an O(n^2) complexity because for each edge in the graph, we potentially update two nodes' distances.

