A circuit breaker is a design pattern that allows you to detect and handle errors or exceptions in your code by wrapping them in a try-catch block. Here's an example of how you can implement a simple circuit breaker in JavaScript:

```javascript
function CircuitBreaker(maxRetries, onReset) {
  this.maxRetries = maxRetries;
  this.attempts = 0;
  this.lastError = null;

  this.try = async function (fn, ...args) {
    try {
      return await fn.apply(this, args);
    } catch (error) {
      if (!this.lastError || !error.message.includes(this.lastError.message)) {
        this.lastError = error;
        this.attempts++;
        if (this.attempts >= maxRetries) {
          onReset();
          throw new Error(`Maximum retries exceeded: ${maxRetries}`);
        }
      } else {
        throw error;
      }
    }
  };
}

// Example usage:
const cb = new CircuitBreaker(3, () => console.log("Circuit breaker reset"));

async function example() {
  try {
    await cb.try(() => 1 / 0); // This will throw a ZeroDivisionError
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Error: ${error.message}`);
  }
}

example();
```

In this example, the `CircuitBreaker` constructor takes two arguments: `maxRetries`, which specifies the maximum number of retries allowed before resetting the circuit breaker, and `onReset`, which is a callback function that will be called when the circuit breaker resets.

The `try` method wraps an asynchronous function in a try-catch block. If the wrapped function throws an error, it checks if this is the first time encountering this particular error (using `lastError`). If not, and if we've reached the maximum number of retries (`maxRetries`), it calls the `onReset` callback and rethrows the original error.

You can customize this implementation to fit your specific use case by modifying the `CircuitBreaker` constructor and the `try` method.

