Here's an example of how you can implement merge sort in C#:

```csharp
using System;

public class MergeSort
{
    public static void Sort(int[] arr)
    {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.Length - 1;

        while (right > left)
        {
            // Base case: If the array has only one element, it's already sorted.
            if (arr.Length == 1)
                break;

            int mid = (left + right) / 2; // Calculate middle index

            // Recursively sort two halves of the array
            Sort(arr, left, mid);
            Sort(arr, mid + 1, right);

            // Merge sorted halves into a single array
            for (int i = left; i <= right; i++)
                arr[i] = Merge(arr, left, mid, mid + 1, right);
        }
    }

    private static int[] Merge(int[] arr, int l, int m, int r)
    {
        // Create a new array to store the merged result
        int[] result = new int[r - l + 1];

        // Copy elements from both halves into the new array
        for (int i = 0; i <= m - l; i++)
            result[i] = arr[l + i];
        for (int i = 0; i <= r - (m + 1); i++)
            result[m + 1 + i] = arr[m + 1 + i];

        // Return the merged array
        return result;
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a recursive approach to sort the array. The `Sort` method calculates the middle index of the current segment and recursively calls itself on both halves until the base case is reached (i.e., when the array has only one element). Then, it merges the two sorted halves into a single array.

The `Merge` method takes in four parameters: the original array, left index, mid index, right index. It creates a new array to store the merged result and copies elements from both halves into this new array. Finally, it returns the merged array.

This implementation has O(n log n) complexity because each element is accessed once during the merge step, and the number of comparisons made during the sorting process is proportional to the number of elements in the array.

