Here's an implementation of Dijkstra's algorithm in TypeScript:

```typescript
interface Node {
  id: number;
  distance: number;
}

type Graph = Record<number, Array<Node>>;

function dijkstra(graph: Graph, startNodeId: number): Node[] {
  const nodes = Object.keys(graph).map((nodeId) => ({
    id: parseInt(nodeId),
    distance: Infinity,
  }));

  const startNode = nodes.find((node) => node.id === startNodeId);
  if (!startNode) throw new Error("Start node not found");

  startNode.distance = 0;

  const heap = [[0, startNode.id]];
  const visitedNodes = new Set();

  while (heap.length > 0) {
    let [distance, nodeId] = heap.shift();

    if (!visitedNodes.has(nodeId)) {
      visitedNodes.add(nodeId);

      const neighbors = graph[nodeId];
      for (const neighbor of neighbors) {
        if (!visitedNodes.has(neighbor.id)) {
          const newDistance = distance + 1;
          if (newDistance < neighbor.distance) {
            heap.push([newDistance, neighbor.id]);
            neighbor.distance = newDistance;
          }
        }
      }
    } else {
      // Node is already visited, skip it
    }
  }

  return nodes.filter((node) => !visitedNodes.has(node.id));
}
```

This implementation uses a priority queue (heap) to keep track of the nodes with their distances. The algorithm starts by initializing all node distances to infinity and setting the start node's distance to 0.

The main loop iterates over the heap, extracting the node with the smallest distance each time. If this node has not been visited yet, it is marked as visited, and its neighbors are processed. For each neighbor, if its distance can be improved (i.e., a shorter path exists), its distance is updated in the heap.

The algorithm returns an array of nodes that have their distances finalized, which represents the shortest paths from the start node to all other nodes.

This implementation has O((E + V) log V) complexity, where E is the number of edges and V is the number of vertices.

